Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nThere are 8 pens along a wall in the pound.  The pound has to allocate 4 pens to dogs, 3 to cats, and one to roosters.  In how many ways can the pound make the allocation?","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"071b560cb90f9ea646a3a5e349cc27ab70d2836a643702fe2a9f048b3969826a"},"seconds":9.087809562683105,"seed":738378501906033298,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the problem of allocating 8 pens to dogs, cats, and roosters, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Choose 4 pens out of 8 for the dogs.**\n   The number of ways to choose 4 pens out of 8 is given by the combination formula \\( \\binom{n}{k} = \\frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!} \\).\n   \\[\n   \\binom{8}{4} = \\frac{8!}{4!4!} = \\frac{8 \\times 7 \\times 6 \\times 5}{4 \\times 3 \\times 2 \\times 1} = 70\n   \\]\n\n2. **Choose 3 pens out of the remaining 4 for the cats.**\n   The number of ways to choose 3 pens out of 4 is:\n   \\[\n   \\binom{4}{3} = \\frac{4!}{3!1!} = \\frac{4}{1} = 4\n   \\]\n\n3. **The last pen automatically goes to the roosters.**\n   There is only 1 way to choose the last pen, which is:\n   \\[\n   \\binom{1}{1} = 1\n   \\]\n\n4. **Multiply the number of ways to get the total number of allocations.**\n   The total number of ways to allocate the pens is the product of the number of ways to choose the pens for dogs, cats, and roosters:\n   \\[\n   70 \\times 4 \\times 1 = 280\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the number of ways the pound can make the allocation is \\(\\boxed{280}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{280}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"counting_and_probability:301"}
